---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import overview from '../../assets/projects/homelab.svg';
---

<Layout title="Home lab">
  <article class="wrap page">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Home lab"
      description="Linux services at home and on Oracle Cloud."
      stack="Linux / Proxmox / Oracle Cloud / Docker Compose"
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/homelab">
        Repository ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/blog/building-my-home-lab">
        Lab notes
      </a>
    </CaseHeader>
    <figure class="case-image">
      <a
        class="case-image-link"
        href={overview.src}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        aria-label="Open Home Lab service overview at full size (new tab)"
      >
        <Image
          src={overview}
          alt="Home and cloud services, remote access and monitoring in my Home Lab"
          format="svg"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Home Lab service overview · October 2026. Select the image to enlarge.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Services</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I maintain Linux services locally and on Oracle Cloud. Docker Compose
          lets me describe services in configuration; Proxmox gives me virtual
          machines for experimentation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pi-hole handles DNS filtering, Tailscale gives me remote access to
          home devices, and Traefik routes requests to services. Wazuh,
          Prometheus and Grafana help me look at logs and system performance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>DNS troubleshooting</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          For DNS troubleshooting, I check which resolver a device is using,
          inspect the Pi-hole query log, then compare a local record with a
          public lookup. That separates a DNS issue from an unavailable
          application.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="source-example"
          tabindex="0"
          aria-label="Scrollable code example"
        ><code># Example diagnostic commands; use your resolver address.
nslookup pi.home.arpa 192.168.1.100
nslookup example.com 192.168.1.100</code></pre>
        <p class="muted">
          These are illustrative commands, not an export of my network
          configuration. The diagram groups services; it is not a map of
          firewall rules or physical connections.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/blog/pihole-dns-filtering">
          Read my DNS notes
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <a class="next-project" href="/projects/glyph">
      <span>Next project</span>
      <strong>
        Glyph <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </strong>
    </a>
  </article>
</Layout>
